Node.js built-in proxy support does not apply to requests made through a Unix domain socket. If an `http.request()` uses the `socketPath` option, proxy settings are ignored.

```js
const http = require('node:http');

http.request({
  socketPath: '/path/to/socket.sock',
  path: '/',
}, (res) => {
  // ...
}).end();
```

`socketPath` is the Unix domain socket option and cannot be used together with `host` or `port`.
